   "text": "The SMILES below was read region by region from the drawing. I did not run it through a parser, but I checked ring closures and atom counts by hand.\n\n**Macrocycle**\n- The left ring is a chlorobenzene. One ortho position carries CH2CH2\u2013O. The other ortho position carries CH2\u2013O\u2013pyridine.\n- The ether O from the CH2CH2 chain connects through a CH2 to the central benzene ring.\n- The central benzene has three substituents: that CH2\u2013O, the 2-pyridyl group, and the CH2 leading to the benzimidazole. The CH2\u2013O and CH2\u2013benzimidazole carbons are ortho to each other, and the pyridyl is para to the CH2\u2013benzimidazole carbon.\n- The pyridine is 2,6-disubstituted, with the ring N between the aryl carbon and the O-bearing carbon, which closes the macrocycle.\n\n**Benzimidazole**\n- C2 carries the CH2 linking it to the central benzene.\n- N1 carries the oxetan-2-ylmethyl group.\n- On the benzo ring, the methyl ester sits on C6 and the OCH2CH2N(CH3)Boc chain on C4. The Boc group is the tert-butyl carbamate.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- The only drawn stereocentre is the oxetane C2, where the bond to CH2N is hashed. With priorities O > CH2N > ring CH2 > H, this gives S.\n- In the SMILES, `n1C[C@@H]1CCO1` encodes S for that carbon.\n\nSMILES: Clc1ccc2c(c1)CCOCc1cc(ccc1Cc1nc3c(n1C[C@@H]1CCO1)cc(cc3OCCN(C)C(=O)OC(C)(C)C)C(=O)OC)-c1cccc(n1)OC2"
